"executive_summary": "Topological valley photonics exploits the valley degree of freedom to control electromagnetic waves, but prior work has been confined to two dimensions, where light confinement along the third dimension is achieved in a non-topological way and suffers from out-of-plane losses, restricting light flow to planar geometries. This paper introduces a three-dimensional (3D) topological valley photonics framework that combines a complete photonic bandgap with vectorial valley-contrasting physics. Unlike 2D systems with a single pair of valleys characterized by scalar valley Chern numbers, the 3D model involves triple pairs of valleys characterized by valley Chern vectors, enabling vectorial bulk valley vortices and canalized chiral valley surface states. The authors develop a 3D photonic crystal with a diamond-like lattice built from perfect electric conductor (PEC) spheres and rods, and break inversion symmetry by using two spheres of different diameters within each face-centered-cubic cell. This gapping produces a complete photonic bandgap and six valleys grouped into three pairs by time-reversal symmetry. Using the k·p method, they derive an effective Hamiltonian near each valley and show that the Berry curvature has nonzero components only in the plane perpendicular to the valley momentum, verifying vectorial valley-contrasting physics. Experimentally, they use a vectorial chiral source array to selectively excite valley states with valley-locked chirality and observe directional outcoupling at 13.8 GHz, confirming valley-momentum locking. They also demonstrate canalized chiral surface states on all surfaces of a shaped sample and reveal a right-hand-thumb rule relating the valley Chern vectors to the circulating propagation direction of the surface states. The findings highlight applications in 3D waveguiding, directional radiation, and imaging, and suggest extension to terahertz and optical frequencies using all-dielectric diamond-like crystals."